<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Keshab Chandra Sen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Keshab Chandra Sen was a Bengali religious and social reformer who led the Brahmo Samaj, campaigning against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, child marriage and for women's education and widow remarriage. He founded the Brahmo Samaj of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1866. The marriage of his under-age daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suniti-devi">Suniti Devi</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Cooch Behar in 1878 split the movement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/keshab-chandra-sen.jpg" alt="Keshab Chandra Sen" width="320" height="526" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: London Stereoscopic Company · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Keshub_Chunder_Sen_(1838-1884).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
